          <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">   Generate image </span><math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{t}\leftarrow\text{T2I}(p^{(t)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">I</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext mathsize="0.800em">T2I</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">p</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{t}\leftarrow\text{T2I}(p^{(t)})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> and obtain DVQ responses: </span><math id="alg1.l9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{(t)}=\texttt{VQA}_{\text{MLLM}}(I^{(t)},Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">R</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace" mathsize="0.800em">VQA</mtext><mtext mathsize="0.800em">MLLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">I</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">Q</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{(t)}=\texttt{VQA}_{\text{MLLM}}(I^{(t)},Q)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l9.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l10.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">   </span><span id="alg1.l10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">(§<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Pairwise Objective and Comparator (Block 6) ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>)</span><span id="alg1.l10.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> Update best prompt/image so far: </span><math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{p^{*}_{:t},I^{*}_{:t}\}\leftarrow\{p^{(t)},I^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">p</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">∗</mo></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">I</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">p</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">I</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p^{*}_{:t},I^{*}_{:t}\}\leftarrow\{p^{(t)},I^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l10.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> if </span><math id="alg1.l10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l10.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">; otherwise </span><math id="alg1.l10.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{p^{*}_{:t},I^{*}_{:t}\}\leftarrow\texttt{Compare}_{\text{MLLM}}\big(\{p^{(t)},I^{(t)}\},\{p^{*}_{:t-1},I^{*}_{:t-1}\}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">p</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">∗</mo></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">I</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em" stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace" mathsize="0.800em">Compare</mtext><mtext mathsize="0.800em">MLLM</mtext></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">p</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">I</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">p</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">∗</mo></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">I</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p^{*}_{:t},I^{*}_{:t}\}\leftarrow\texttt{Compare}_{\text{MLLM}}\big(\{p^{(t)},I^{(t)}\},\{p^{*}_{:t-1},I^{*}_{:t-1}\}\big)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l10.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l11.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><span id="alg1.l11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">end</span><span id="alg1.l11.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><span id="alg1.l11.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l12.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><span id="alg1.l12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">return</span><span id="alg1.l12.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">  Best prompt/image at termination </span><math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{p^{*},I^{*}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">p</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">∗</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">I</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p^{*},I^{*}\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l12.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> after </span><math id="alg1.l12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l12.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> steps or </span><math id="alg1.l12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l12.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> non-improving steps.
</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:172.5pt;">
      
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<div id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:172.5pt;height:182.5pt;vertical-align:-90.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-35.9pt,38.0pt) scale(0.706131731105085,0.706131731105085) ;">
<table id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_t">
<span id="S3.p1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:227.6pt;">
<span id="S3.p1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">User prompt <math id="S3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{u}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span id="S3.p1.1.1.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">: studio close-up shot of an antique book with knowledge is power painted in gold on the cover in thick flowing brushed calligraphy</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.p1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_t">
<span id="S3.p1.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:227.6pt;">
<span id="S3.p1.1.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.1.1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">Initial LLM proposal</span><span id="S3.p1.1.1.2.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> </span><math id="S3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p1.1.1.2.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">: Studio close-up photograph of an antique leather-bound book lying open on a polished mahogany table. The cover, illuminated by a soft Rembrandt-esque light from the upper left, displays the phrase "Knowledge is Power" painted in thick, flowing, gold leaf calligraphy reminiscent of medieval illuminated manuscripts. The edges of the book are worn and slightly frayed, hinting at age and frequent use. Dust motes dance in the beam of light, adding a touch of atmospheric depth. The background is a slightly out-of-focus dark oak bookshelf filled with similarly aged tomes, creating a sense of scholarly reverence.</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.p1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_t">
<span id="S3.p1.1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:227.6pt;"><img src="2509.10704v1/figs/qualitative_analysis/158_0-prompt_0-batch_0.png" id="S3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:115/115;" width="115" height="115" alt="[Uncaptioned image]"><img src="2509.10704v1/figs/qualitative_analysis/158_0-prompt_1-batch_0.png" id="S3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:115/115;" width="115" height="115" alt="[Uncaptioned image]">
</span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</div>
      
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.14" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.15" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">Illustrating examples of an user prompt <math id="S3.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding initial LLM proposal prompt <math id="S3.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> and their corresponding generations using Imagen 3 (<span id="S3.F2.15.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">left</span>: <math id="S3.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>; <span id="S3.F2.15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">right</span>: <math id="S3.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Refer to additional illustrations in App. <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 Additional Qualitative Results ‣ Appendix B Additional Experiments ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
    
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present <span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Maestro</span> (standing for <span id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">M</span>ulti-<span id="S3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">A</span>gent <span id="S3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Orch</span>estration and highlighting it as a <span id="S3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">maestro</span> for image generation), our proposed framework for automated prompt optimization in T2I generation. The high-level workflow of <span id="S3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Maestro</span> is illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, and a detailed pseudo-code representation is provided in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Following the taxonomy discussed in §<a href="#S2" title="2 Related Work ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <span id="S3.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Maestro</span> primarily adopts a test-time optimization framework. This design choice circumvents the need for large-scale labeled training data and ensures that our method is model-agnostic, capable of working with various black-box T2I generators and (M)LLM optimizers. While test-time iteration inherently increases runtime compared to one-shot generation that might be utilizing train-time approaches, it mirrors the iterative refinement process users often undertake manually to achieve satisfactory results, but it is worth emphasizing that <span id="S3.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Maestro</span><span id="S3.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> features completely autonomous self-improvement</span> and, by default, requires only initial user prompt as the manual component. Furthermore, as we will discuss below, our framework is designed to seamlessly integrate and benefit from train-time approaches by potentially using their output during its initialization phase, thereby unifying the strengths of both paradigms. While we provide a high-level walkthrough in the captions of Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we discuss each key component of <span id="S3.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Maestro</span> in detail below.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Initialization (Blocks 1-3 in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an initial user prompt <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Maestro</span> performs two key operations once at the beginning of the process to establish a robust starting point and necessary components for subsequent iterations:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generating initial LLM proposal</span> (Block 3): As discussed in §<a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, user prompts often lack the structure or clarity that facilitates optimal T2I generation. Recognizing that some improvements can be achieved by aligning the prompt with general prompt engineering best practices, we first employ a text-LLM (Gemini 1.5 Pro in our experiments) guided by a meta-prompt containing established guidelines (e.g., focusing on clear subject description, defining context and scene details, specifying artistic style; see App. <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Implementation Details ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for details) to transform the raw user prompt <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> into an initial, potentially more effective prompt <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math>. The objective here is twofold: 1) to achieve a better starting point for the iterative process, potentially reducing the number of iterations needed, and 2) to provide a natural bridge between train-time and test-time methods: while we currently use a general instruction-following LLM, this step could readily incorporate a specialized prompt optimization model fine-tuned via a train-time approach such as one outlined in §<a href="#S2" title="2 Related Work ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, if available. We show an illustrative example in Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> where we compare <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> against <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> and their corresponding generated images, and we find that while simplistic, the initial LLM rewrite is already surprisingly effective in enriching the text prompt with descriptive information and in enhancing the resultant generation (e.g., compared to the image generated by <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, the image generated by <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> features text that is correctly painted on the cover, and the book has a better antique appearance), although there is still room for improvement (e.g., the font of the phrase is still not in ‘thick flowing brushed calligraphy’ requested by the user).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generating decomposed visual questions (DVQs)</span> (Block 2): Concurrently, we leverage techniques previously developed for T2I evaluation to decompose the initial user prompt <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> into a set of DVQs, each probing a desirable property implied by the prompt via an LLM call <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cho et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yarom et al., 2023</a>, inter alia)</cite>. Specifically, we use a pipeline inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cho et al. (2024)</a></cite> but instead of relying on in-context learning only, we additionally prompt the LLM once more to refine on the initial questions to improve coherence and precision. For example, the user prompt in Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> yields the following DVQs (<math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\leftarrow\texttt{DVQGen}_{\text{LLM}}(p_{u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">DVQGen</mtext><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\leftarrow\texttt{DVQGen}_{\text{LLM}}(p_{u})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{Q}=\{q_{1},...,q_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{Q}=\{q_{1},...,q_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of DVQs and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{DVQGen}_{\text{LLM}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">DVQGen</mtext><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{DVQGen}_{\text{LLM}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> refers to the operation of generating DVQs by prompting an LLM with <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>):</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Is the image a close-up of the book?</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Is there a book?</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Is the book antique?</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Does the cover appear to be part of an antique book? (e.g., material, wear, binding style)</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Does the cover say “knowledge is power”?</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">6.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Is the “knowledge is power” text painted in gold?</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">7.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Does the “knowledge is power” text appear to be made with thick brushstrokes?</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">8.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Does the “knowledge is power” text have a flowing calligraphic style?</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i9" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">9.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Does the lighting appear to be studio quality (e.g., even, controlled)?</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i10" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">10.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Is the background uncluttered and consistent with a studio setting?</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">These DVQs are reused at multiple stages within our pipeline, but importantly, unlike the aforementioned work that primarily uses the responses to these questions to evaluate prompt-image consistency, we do <span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">not</span> use the scalar, aggregated answer correctness of these DVQs as the optimization objective. While convenient, simple aggregation (e.g., summing or averaging binary scores) dilutes the rich, multifaceted information captured by the individual questions and struggles to accurately represent complex user preferences, often showing poor correlation with human judgments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ross et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. This also deviates from how humans typically form preferences through comparative assessment. Instead, <span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Maestro</span> optimizes directly based on pairwise preferences generated by an LLM judge, as detailed next.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Pairwise Objective and Comparator (Block 6)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=\{0,1,...\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=\{0,1,...\}</annotation></semantics></math>-th iteration with the current prompt proposal <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> (when <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math>, it is the initial LLM proposal mentioned above; for <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, it is generated via the techniques to be outlined in §<a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Generating New Prompt Proposals (Blocks 7-9) ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>), we generate corresponding image(s) by calling the T2I model: <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{(t)}\leftarrow\texttt{T2I}(p^{(t)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">T2I</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{(t)}\leftarrow\texttt{T2I}(p^{(t)})</annotation></semantics></math> (Block 4). We then follow the standard VQA pipeline to evaluate <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> against the list of DVQs generated during initialization to obtain the responses with an MLLM: <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R^{(t)}\leftarrow\{r_{i}:=\texttt{VQA}_{\text{MLLM}}(I^{(t)},q_{i})\,\forall q_{i}\in Q\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">VQA</mtext><mtext>MLLM</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{(t)}\leftarrow\{r_{i}:=\texttt{VQA}_{\text{MLLM}}(I^{(t)},q_{i})\,\forall q_{i}\in Q\}</annotation></semantics></math> where we follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al. (2023)</a></cite> to define the response as the probability of the “Yes” token given the image and the DVQs <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}^{(t)}:=p_{\texttt{MLLM}}(\text{``Yes''}|I^{(t)},q_{i})\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">MLLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>“Yes”</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}^{(t)}:=p_{\texttt{MLLM}}(\text{``Yes''}|I^{(t)},q_{i})\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> and store the 3-tuple <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{p^{(t)},I^{(t)},R^{(t)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p^{(t)},I^{(t)},R^{(t)}\}</annotation></semantics></math> (Block 5) in <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comparator</span> (Block 6), a core component of <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Maestro</span>, where we adopt the <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pairwise preference from the LLM feedback</span> to track the <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">best prompt &amp; image up to the <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th iteration</span> (denoted as <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p^{*}_{:t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}_{:t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I^{*}_{:t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{*}_{:t}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatleft" style="width:44%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.10704v1/pairwise.drawio.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/149;" width="215" height="149"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustration of the binary tournament performed by Comparator (Block 6) to obtain the best image so far at <math id="S3.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th iteration <math id="S3.F3.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I^{*}_{:t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{*}_{:t}</annotation></semantics></math> given new proposal(s) <math id="S3.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and the previous best <math id="S3.F3.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I^{*}_{:t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{*}_{:t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rationale.</span> As mentioned, evaluating generative images is inherently subjective, multifaceted and is without a reference ground-truth, which we argue closely parallels the problem addressed by RLHF and reinforcement learning with AI feedback (RLAIF, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al. (2024)</a></cite>), where human/AI-annotated pairwise preferences are used to effectively align models with complex, hard-to-quantify human values or instructions – goals where assigning a consistent, absolute scoring function is notoriously difficult. However, whereas RLHF/RLAIF typically trains a reward model to ultimately approximate the pairwise preference to a scalar value through, for example, learning a Bradley-Terry model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bradley and Terry, 1952</a>)</cite>, we propose to optimize against the pairwise objective directly as elicited by the MLLM-as-a-judge in iteration, bypassing the potential inaccuracies, biases and limitations introduced by an intermediate reward model approximation through a procedure we outline below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Procedure.</span> Directly optimizing against a pairwise objective requires a reference for comparison. At <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th iteration, the Comparator (<math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{Compare}_{\text{MLLM}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">Compare</mtext><mtext>MLLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{Compare}_{\text{MLLM}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>) determines the best prompt/generation by comparing the latest candidate prompt/generation against the <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">incumbent</span> best:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{p^{*}_{:t},I^{*}_{:t}\}\leftarrow\texttt{Compare}_{\text{MLLM}}\Big(\{p^{(t)},I^{(t)}\},\{p^{*}_{:t-1},I^{*}_{:t-1}\}\Big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">Compare</mtext><mtext>MLLM</mtext></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p^{*}_{:t},I^{*}_{:t}\}\leftarrow\texttt{Compare}_{\text{MLLM}}\Big(\{p^{(t)},I^{(t)}\},\{p^{*}_{:t-1},I^{*}_{:t-1}\}\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">To initialize, we assign <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p^{*}_{:0}\leftarrow p^{(0)},I^{*}_{:0}\leftarrow I^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>I</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}_{:0}\leftarrow p^{(0)},I^{*}_{:0}\leftarrow I^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> (the <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math> row in Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2 Pairwise Objective and Comparator (Block 6) ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). In subsequent iterations <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we conduct a side-by-side “duel” between <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I^{*}_{:t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{*}_{:t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, conditional on the <span id="S3.SS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">user prompt</span> <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> with a capable MLLM (we use Gemini 1.5 Pro in our experiments) prompted as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="307.07" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 676.77 307.07" width="676.77"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,307.07) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#A1C6EA;" fill="#A1C6EA" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 7.87 L 0 299.2 C 0 303.54 3.53 307.07 7.87 307.07 L 668.9 307.07 C 673.25 307.07 676.77 303.54 676.77 299.2 L 676.77 7.87 C 676.77 3.53 673.25 0 668.9 0 L 7.87 0 C 3.53 0 0 3.53 0 7.87 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F7F9FC;" fill="#F7F9FC" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 9.84 L 1.97 282.96 L 674.8 282.96 L 674.8 9.84 C 674.8 5.49 671.28 1.97 666.93 1.97 L 9.84 1.97 C 5.49 1.97 1.97 5.49 1.97 9.84 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 291.55)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:39.81em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="550.86"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:39.81em;">
<span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">MLLM-as-a-judge prompt for binary tournament</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 13.78)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:53.86em;--ltx-fo-height:18.6em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="257.37" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 257.37)" width="745.27"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:53.86em;">
<span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">You are an expert in critiquing images. Given a user prompt, a text-to-image model generated two images (“Image A” and “Image B”), and your task is to pick the overall better image. The user prompt is:</span></span>
<span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">“{ <span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">user_prompt</span> }”</span></span>
<span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Image A:
{ <span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">image_A</span> }</span></span>
<span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Image B:
{ <span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">image_B</span> }</span></span>
<span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Please carefully explain the rationale <span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">before</span> giving your final choice. Make sure you finish your answer with “<span id="S3.SS2.p4.pic1.2.5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;answer&gt; X &lt;/answer&gt;</span>”, where X is “A” or “B”.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2 Pairwise Objective and Comparator (Block 6) ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, if more than one image is generated in one iteration, we conduct a tournament using this pairwise comparison to identify the single best candidate among them before comparing it against <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I^{*}_{:t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{*}_{:t}</annotation></semantics></math>. To improve the robustness of the comparison and in particular, to reduce <span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">position bias</span> inherent in LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chiang and Lee, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, we query the MLLM judge <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math> times (with <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I^{*}_{:t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{*}_{:t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> each appearing as <span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">image_A</span> <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> times) in parallel with temperature of 0.7 and break ties randomly to select the final winner, and finally assign <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I^{*}_{:t}\leftarrow I^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>I</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{*}_{:t}\leftarrow I^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> wins the duel or <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I^{*}_{:t}\leftarrow I^{*}_{:t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>I</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{*}_{:t}\leftarrow I^{*}_{:t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise. This iterative process of proposal, generation, and pairwise comparison continues until a predefined budget (e.g., maximum number of T2I calls <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>) is exhausted, or optionally, if <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{p^{*}_{:t},I^{*}_{:t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p^{*}_{:t},I^{*}_{:t}\}</annotation></semantics></math> do not change for <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> consecutive iterations (patience criterion). The comparator then returns the final results <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{p^{*},I^{*}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p^{*},I^{*}\}</annotation></semantics></math> to the users.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Generating New Prompt Proposals (Blocks 7-9)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The mechanism by which new prompts are proposed based on prior evaluations lies at the heart of any iterative APO algorithm: <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Maestro</span> employs a dual-generator strategy, leveraging two distinct approaches to create candidate prompts for the next iteration upon the best prompt-image pairs so far <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{p^{*}_{:t},I^{*}_{:t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p^{*}_{:t},I^{*}_{:t}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. This design acknowledges the multifaceted nature of T2I evaluation and allows us to capitalize on different types of feedback signals. The two generators are designed to be complementary: the first, which we term “<span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">targeted editing</span>”, explicitly targets specific defects identified via structured feedback on previous generations, while the second, which we term “<span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">implicit improvement</span>”, aims for holistic improvements based on MLLM judge’s general multimodal understanding capabilities.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.SS3.tab1" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S3.SS3.tab1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:452.9pt;vertical-align:-227.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-176.0pt,231.1pt) scale(0.494921661131959,0.494921661131959) ;">
<table id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:199.2pt;">
<span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Current image <math id="S3.SS3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I^{*}_{:t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{*}_{:t-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:256.1pt;">
<span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">Selected DVQ(s), rationalization &amp; suggestions</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:199.2pt;">
<span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">New prompt &amp; generation <math id="S3.SS3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{p^{(t)},I^{(t)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p^{(t)},I^{(t)}\}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_t">
<span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:199.2pt;"><img src="2509.10704v1/figs/qualitative_analysis/158_2-prompt_0-batch_0.png" id="S3.SS3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:381/381;" width="381" height="381" alt="[Uncaptioned image]">
</span></td>
<td id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_t">
<span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:256.1pt;">
<span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Question</span>: Does the "knowledge is power" text appear to be made with thick brushstrokes? 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.2.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Rationalization</span>: The reviewer likely answered "No" <span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.2.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">because the "Knowledge is Power" text appears to be written with a relatively thin, elegant script, rather than thick brushstrokes</span>. While the letters are stylized, they maintain a smooth, flowing quality, resembling calligraphy or penmanship more than the textured, impasto effect of thick paint applied with a brush… 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.2.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suggestion</span>: … the text should be re-rendered to appear as if created with thick brushstrokes. This could be achieved by <span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.2.2.1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">adding texture to the letters, making the edges less precise and more irregular, and incorporating variations in the gold color’s density to simulate the uneven application of paint</span>…</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_t">
<span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:199.2pt;"><img src="2509.10704v1/figs/qualitative_analysis/158_6-prompt_1-batch_0.png" id="S3.SS3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:381/381;" width="381" height="381" alt="[Uncaptioned image]">
<br class="ltx_break">
<span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">… features the phrase "Knowledge is Power" painted in <span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">thick, impasto gold calligraphy, with visible brushstrokes, ridges, and uneven texture, as if applied with a thick brush</span> …</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">
<span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:199.2pt;"><img src="2509.10704v1/figs/qualitative_analysis/968_2-prompt_1-batch_0.png" id="S3.SS3.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:381/381;" width="381" height="381" alt="[Uncaptioned image]">
</span></td>
<td id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">
<span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:256.1pt;">
<span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Questions</span>: Are the apples square? / Are the leaves circular? 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.3.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Rationalization</span>: The reviewer answered "No" because <span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.3.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">the question specifically asks about a square shape, and the provided image depicts a typical, naturally round apple</span> / The reviewer answered "No" because the <span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.3.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">leaves on the apple tree are ovate or elliptical, not circular.</span>… 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.3.2.1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suggestion</span>: * To satisfy the prompt and elicit a "Yes" response, the apple needs to be depicted as square. <span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.3.2.1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">This could be achieved by generating an image of a cuboid apple hanging from a branch</span>… / To create an image where the leaves are circular, the leaf shape needs to be fundamentally altered. <span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.3.2.1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The prompt should include phrases like "perfectly round leaves", "circular foliage", or "leaves shaped like discs", … Using terms like "coin-shaped leaves" can further reinforce the desired circularity.</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t">
<span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.3.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:199.2pt;"><img src="2509.10704v1/figs/qualitative_analysis/968_4-prompt_1-batch_0.png" id="S3.SS3.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:381/381;" width="381" height="381" alt="[Uncaptioned image]">
<br class="ltx_break">
<span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.3.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">Square, visibly seamed red apples, <span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.3.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">carved into cuboid shapes</span>, hanging from a tree with <span id="S3.SS3.tab1.1.1.3.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">perfectly round, disc-shaped, coin-shaped green leaves. Circular foliage. Leaves shaped like discs.</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
</table>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S3.F4.11" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustrating examples of the targeted editing pipeline (§<a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Generating New Prompt Proposals (Blocks 7-9) ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>) with 1) current best image in the step <span id="S3.F4.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">before</span> applying targeted editing; 2) selected DVQ(s) where the judge model answered “No” and excerpts of LLM-generated rationalization and suggestions; and 3) the prompt excerpt and generated image <span id="S3.F4.11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">after</span> incorporating the suggestions (refer to App. <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 Additional Qualitative Results ‣ Appendix B Additional Experiments ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a> for the full prompts). Key parts of rationalization, suggestion and resultingrevised prompt are <span id="S3.F4.11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bolded</span>. The initial user prompts <math id="S3.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> are <span id="S3.F4.11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">studio close-up shot of an antique book with knowledge is power painted in gold on the cover in thick flowing brushed calligraphy</span> (<span id="S3.F4.11.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">top row</span>) and <span id="S3.F4.11.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">square red apples on a tree with circular green leaves</span> (<span id="S3.F4.11.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">bottom row</span>), respectively. Figure is better viewed in color.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Targeted editing (Blocks 7-8).</span> Aiming to mimic the human process of identifying <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">specific</span> deficiencies in a generated image and making targeted adjustments to the prompt to correct them, this generator reuses the DVQs generated at initialization <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and the MLLM’s responses to them for the best image so far <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R^{*}_{:t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{*}_{:t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast to prior works like <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mañas et al. (2024)</a></cite> that primarily used the binary “Yes/No” responses and their aggregated scores as the optimization objective, we propose to use them <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">beyond</span> numerical feedback by noticing two key observations: First, we note that a key advantage of VQA-based scoring is their inherent <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">interpretability</span>, where each question <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}\in Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}\in Q</annotation></semantics></math>, such as the example given in §<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Initialization (Blocks 1-3 in Fig. ) ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, corresponds to a specific, desired characteristic that is human-interpretable. Second, a key forte of modern (M)LLMs is their <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reasoning capabilities</span> in explaining <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">why</span> they made a certain response – such a textual, non-numerical feedback has shown to be useful both to improve the usefulness of the eventual numerical output as a reward signal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mahan et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and, as <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">textual gradients</span>, to outperform numerical feedback-only APO in the textual tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pryzant et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuksekgonul et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. To capitalize on these observations, we <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1)</span> identify a subset of DVQs where the MLLM judge provided a “No” answer (i.e., <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r^{*}_{i,:t-1}=p_{\texttt{MLLM}}(\text{``Yes''}|I^{*}_{:t-1},q_{i})&lt;0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">MLLM</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>“Yes”</mtext><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{*}_{i,:t-1}=p_{\texttt{MLLM}}(\text{``Yes''}|I^{*}_{:t-1},q_{i})&lt;0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I^{*}_{:t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{*}_{:t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> failed to meet a criterion); <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2)</span> prompt the MLLM to generate a <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">textual rationalization</span> explaining <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">why</span> the image failed according to that question to provide an interpretable insight into the failure mode; and <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3)</span> conditional on the rationalization, a specific edit or rewrite to the prompt aimed <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">precisely</span> at rectifying the identified shortcoming – we illustrate this process with an example in Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.3 Generating New Prompt Proposals (Blocks 7-9) ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (with additional qualitative examples in App. <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 Additional Qualitative Results ‣ Appendix B Additional Experiments ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>) and show the detailed prompt to achieve the above in App. <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Implementation Details ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implicit Improvement (Block 9).</span> Complementary to the <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">explicit</span> targeted editing approach above where we target specific inadequacies, we also employ a second generator to focus on <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">implicit</span> holistic enhancement where we prompt a powerful MLLM to assess the current best image (<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I^{*}_{:t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{*}_{:t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>) more broadly in the context of the prompts (<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p^{*}_{:t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}_{:t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>) and to provide prompt improvement without being strictly tied to the predefined DVQs. To achieve so, we incorporate the prompting subroutine introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024)</a></cite> to leverage the MLLM optimizer for general visual critique – but a key distinction is that we apply it always to the best image so far <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I^{*}_{:t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{*}_{:t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of the last generation <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024)</a></cite>, given that our comparator now explicitly tracks the optimization progress. We again refer the readers to App. <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Implementation Details ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for the detailed prompt used.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>User Intent Grounding via Self-Verification (Block 10)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One noticeable differentiator of our prompt generators in §<a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Generating New Prompt Proposals (Blocks 7-9) ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> is the encouragement of <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">non-semantically</span> equivalent edits (as contrasted to general, semantically equivalent paraphrasing) – this is generally beneficial: as exemplified in Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.3 Generating New Prompt Proposals (Blocks 7-9) ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, it often enriches the prompt with concrete instructions, new concepts or descriptive details that T2I models typically respond well to. A risk, nonetheless, is that this process may also lead to edits that deviate from the user’s original intent specified in <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> – for example, when a user enters a fantastical prompt, we find that through reasoning, the LLM may conclude that the requested generation is illogical or otherwise contradicts its world knowledge and proposes a “fix” that fundamentally differs from the user’s original intent. This potential for ‘semantic drift’ can be further compounded by the iterative editing approach that <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Maestro</span> takes, where modifications build upon previous ones over multiple steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this and ensure enhancements do not override the user’s core requirement, we incorporate a final, dedicated “Verify and Self-Correct” block as a regularizer to 1) <span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">detect</span> whether there are core concept violations in the generated prompts from §<a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Generating New Prompt Proposals (Blocks 7-9) ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> and 2) <span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">correct</span> violations, if any: specifically, we use the template below iteratively:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS4.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="242.19" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 676.77 242.19" width="676.77"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,242.19) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FF9393;" fill="#FF9393" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 7.87 L 0 234.31 C 0 238.66 3.53 242.19 7.87 242.19 L 668.9 242.19 C 673.25 242.19 676.77 238.66 676.77 234.31 L 676.77 7.87 C 676.77 3.53 673.25 0 668.9 0 L 7.87 0 C 3.53 0 0 3.53 0 7.87 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFDEDE;" fill="#FFDEDE" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 9.84 L 1.97 218.08 L 674.8 218.08 L 674.8 9.84 C 674.8 5.49 671.28 1.97 666.93 1.97 L 9.84 1.97 C 5.49 1.97 1.97 5.49 1.97 9.84 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 226.67)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:39.81em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="550.86"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS4.p3.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:39.81em;">
<span id="S3.SS4.p3.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p3.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Verify &amp; Self-correct</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 15.93)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:53.86em;--ltx-fo-height:13.76em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.16em;font-size:10pt;" height="192.49" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 190.34)" width="745.27"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS4.p3.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:53.86em;">
<span id="S3.SS4.p3.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p3.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Your task is to first verify whether my original prompt satisfies each of the constraints specified by the user: <span id="S3.SS4.p3.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{ constraints }</span></span></span>
<span id="S3.SS4.p3.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p3.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Make sure you verify <span id="S3.SS4.p3.pic1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">each</span> constraint and answer "Yes" if the constraint is met or "No" otherwise, followed by a short explanation. If there is any unmet constraint, please revise the prompt to satisfy all constraints. Make sure you only modify the part of the prompt that previously led to constraint violations and bracket any revised prompt between <span id="S3.SS4.p3.pic1.2.2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;answer&gt;</span> and <span id="S3.SS4.p3.pic1.2.2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;/answer&gt;</span>. If the original prompt satisfies all constraints, you may simply write "<span id="S3.SS4.p3.pic1.2.2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;answer&gt; NO_CHANGE &lt;/answer&gt;</span>".</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <span id="S3.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{ constraints }</span> are reused DVQs from Block 2 and this refinement operation is repeated iteratively until either "<span id="S3.SS4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;answer&gt; NO_CHANGE &lt;/answer&gt;</span>" is generated or a pre-defined patience (we set to 3 by default) is exhausted. With reference to Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, at the end of this step, the newly generated prompt proposals (Step 10) are routed back to the T2I model and the steps repeat until convergence or any other user-defined stopping criteria (e.g., if the best prompt &amp; image generated so far <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p^{*}_{:t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{*}_{:t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I^{*}_{:t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{*}_{:t}</annotation></semantics></math> do not change after certain number of iterations).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F5.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.10704v1/p2-hard-agg.svg" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/278;" width="476" height="278"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F5.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.10704v1/dsg-1k-agg.svg" id="S4.F5.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/278;" width="476" height="278"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F5.fig3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.10704v1/distribution_p2.svg" id="S4.F5.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/298;" width="476" height="298"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F5.fig4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.10704v1/distribution_dsg.svg" id="S4.F5.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/298;" width="476" height="298"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.13" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S4.F5.14" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Automatic side-by-side (AutoSxS) evaluation results between <span id="S4.F5.14.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Maestro</span> and the baseline methods in <span id="S4.F5.14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(left column)</span> p2-hard and <span id="S4.F5.14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(right column)</span> DSG-1K datasets using a Gemini 2.0 Flash autorater. <span id="S4.F5.14.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Top row</span> shows the win-tie-lose breakdown and the <span id="S4.F5.14.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">bottom row</span> shows the AutoSxS advantage (i.e., # autorater preferred <span id="S4.F5.14.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Maestro</span>- # autorater preferred the baseline) distribution: ranging from -10 (<span id="S4.F5.14.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Maestro</span> is preferred in <span id="S4.F5.14.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">none</span> of the 10 trials) to 10 (<span id="S4.F5.14.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Maestro</span> is preferred in <span id="S4.F5.14.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">all</span> 10 trials) and 0 denotes the autorater is overall neutral; the dashed vertical line denote the mean AutoSxS advantages across all samples.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets and models.</span> We employed the state-of-the-art Imagen 3 T2I model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baldridge et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, available at Google Cloud. Our evaluation was conducted on two datasets:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PartiPrompts</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2022</a>)</cite> comprises a curated collection of challenging user prompts. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mañas et al. (2024)</a></cite>, we focused our experiments on the samples belonging to the most challenging splits (i.e., “fine-grained details” and “complex”; this subset is referred to as <span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">p2-hard</span> hereafter).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DSG-1K</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cho et al., 2024</a>)</cite> is a curated dataset which itself is sampled and aggregated from several widely recognized benchmarks, including TIFA 160 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, DiffusionDB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022</a>)</cite> / MidJourney-prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Turc and Nemade, 2022</a>)</cite> (both of which were crowd-sourced and curated from real user queries), and PoseScript <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Delmas et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, among others.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recognizing the inherent strength of Imagen 3, we further implemented an additional filtering step to ensure that any observed improvements attributable different optimization strategies were not merely the result of stochastic fluctuations. For each initial prompt, with the fixed prompt, we generated images 8 times. We then used DSGScore <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cho et al., 2024</a>)</cite> as a preliminary quality assessment tool to score each of the generated images, and we perform filtering to retain only the samples for which the model failed to achieve a perfect DSGScore of 1.0 in <span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">any</span> of the eight attempts. This strategy allowed us to focus on the more challenging scenarios where the model could not achieve optimal performance through repeated sampling alone. Finally, unless stated otherwise, we used Gemini 1.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reid et al., 2024</a>)</cite> for all cases where an (M)LLM is requested (although we also experimented generation with additional models in App. <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Different Optimizer Models ‣ Appendix B Additional Experiments ‣ Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Mean <math id="S4.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> standard deviation of DSGScore and rank of various methods using Gemini 2.0 Flash as the judge model. <math id="S4.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math>: higher is better; <math id="S4.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>: lower is better.</span></figcaption>
<div id="S4.T1.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:276.0pt;height:53.2pt;vertical-align:-25.2pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-114.3pt,22.0pt) scale(0.547062979126081,0.547062979126081) ;">
